Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Lumen Spire**, a slim beacon spire whose halo ring
spins while its solar lens pulses up and down, as a 46x90x46 opaque-voxel model using
only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting discrete opaque cells** and you
**author its two self-playing animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the Spire is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you break
the spire into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape every form
are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a spinning, pulsing beacon spire
needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` —
together with the rig you define are your submission.

You paint **discrete opaque cells**: `set-voxel` places a single cell, `fill-box`,
`stroke-box`, 3D lines, and spheres lay down runs of cells, and a mirror plane
reflects the ones you place. Every voxel operation targets a **part** with
`--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log, in the shared volume's coordinates. The
volume starts **empty**. Voxels are **opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no
transparency. Each call **records only** — it appends the operation to that part's log
and renders nothing; run `voxel-anim render` when you want to (re)draw the per-part
previews (`parts/<part>.png`) and the assembled scene.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each
a parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the
required animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every field operation
targets a part with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name tower
voxel-anim fill-box --part tower --x0 16 --y0 0 --z0 16 --x1 27 --y1 72 --z1 27 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining
parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Run
`voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to see that part's progress **and
the assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole spire composed from all your parts,
drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the halo ring encircles the spire's crown and the lens
seats atop its tip. Because each part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates,
position each part where it belongs on the finished spire; the scene simply composes
them there.

