## What to deliver

Build and rig the spire up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png` matches
the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together correctly,
and the rig is complete: a slim masonry spire tower, a halo ring around its crown, and
a solar lens atop its tip. **Author the two required animations** as F-curves with the
`voxel-anim` animation subcommands: `halo_ring_spin` (a full, continuous revolution of
the ring about the spire's vertical axis, wrapping seamlessly as it loops) and
`lens_pulse` (a rise-and-settle bob that eases up off the seat and settles back down
with weight, not a linear bounce). Both are **self-playing** idles: they run
continuously on their own, with no caller. The animations are the fixed contract —
produce both, by those names; the parts and joints that realize them are yours. Before you finish, run `voxel-anim render` so it emits the per-part
`.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part is scored as empty
(`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one part; `voxel-anim render --time
<ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant of an animation to
check the motion). When
you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are your finished
submission.
